1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:10,925
The American economy grew in both quarters of 2026 we have numbers for, and yet
by September the average hourly paycheck bought less than it had a year before.

2
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:14,146
So is this a boom, or are
workers going backwards?

3
00:00:14,467 --> 00:00:17,179
Which side is right depends
on which number you look at.

4
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:25,550
Donald Trump has put one side plainly: "The economy
is the greatest economy we've ever had by far."

5
00:00:25,567 --> 00:00:35,779
The other side points at the paycheck, and CBS News put it bluntly: inflation "is really
just eating up all of the wage growth that Trump has talked about and then some".

6
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:40,012
Start with the boom: how fast is
the economy actually growing?

7
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:53,633
Real GDP, the value of everything the country produces after stripping out price rises, grew
at an annual rate of 2.5 percent in the first quarter of 2026 and 2.2 percent in the second.

8
00:00:53,633 --> 00:01:04,858
That is solid growth, but Federal Reserve officials put the economy's long-run pace at 2.0
percent a year, so this runs only a little above normal, not the surge the word boom suggests.

9
00:01:05,867 --> 00:01:07,687
What about jobs?

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,138
The unemployment rate was 4.2 percent in September, and
it has stayed between 4.1 and 4.3 percent since March.

11
00:01:16,167 --> 00:01:24,267
At its September press conference, the Federal Reserve's chair said "the
unemployment rate is basically running consistent with full employment".

12
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:30,517
But payrolls barely moved: employers
added just 29,000 jobs in September.

13
00:01:30,533 --> 00:01:38,233
And the jobs report itself never defines full employment, so
calling this full employment is a judgement, not a measurement.

14
00:01:39,233 --> 00:01:42,833
Now the paycheck, the number
most people actually feel.

15
00:01:43,133 --> 00:01:51,158
In the year to January 2026, pay beat prices, and average
hourly earnings after inflation were up 1.2 percent.

16
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:57,942
Then prices jumped in the spring, and from April on, real
hourly pay was lower than a year earlier in every month.

17
00:01:57,967 --> 00:02:07,629
By September, pay was up 3.0 percent on the year and prices
3.7 percent, leaving real hourly earnings 0.7 percent lower.

18
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:16,958
Trump's boast that wages are "rising much faster than inflation"
matched the year to January; it does not match the year to September.

19
00:02:16,967 --> 00:02:24,292
One limit: these are averages across all employees, so they cannot
tell you how the worker in the middle of the pay scale did.

20
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:38,675
So is the economy booming? By these measures, no: it is growing a little faster than the Fed's long-run
pace, with unemployment right at the level Fed officials expect it to settle at, but slow hiring.

21
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:50,675
Growth and shrinking real pay are not a contradiction: in 2026 they ran side by
side, because output, jobs and what a paycheck buys are different measurements.

22
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:57,525
So the next time someone tells you the economy is booming,
or that it is broken, ask one question: which number?
